**Read:** [Solid State Physics — Steven H. Simon, 2012 draft](../sources/Solid-State-Physics-Simon-2012.lingua#r_3R4Q71S31MD0EH6YVDQQ2ESKH5), Drude, Sommerfeld and band discussions; [Solid State Physics — David Tong](../sources/Solid-State-Physics-Tong.lingua#r_2G2CQ9C5QDEBTQ2YNMYQ5QK3MB), electron dynamics; [Nano-to-Macro Transport Processes — MIT 2.57](../../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/sources/Nano-to-Macro-Transport-MIT-2-57.lingua#r_1YF7TD689ZRGQ51D4B3NGVHZQG), carrier scattering. Compare these assumptions with the functional Cambridge readings.

A perfect periodic lattice is already included in a Bloch state; resistivity is not explained by repeatedly treating each identical lattice site as a random obstacle. Thermal motion, impurities, defects, boundaries and interactions provide distinct scattering mechanisms. Adding separate resistivities is an approximation with conditions, especially when mechanisms interact.

Amorphous materials, disordered semiconductors and conductive polymers may require localized states, hopping, percolation or other descriptions. Fitting an activated resistance does not uniquely prove a band gap. Grain boundaries, contacts and chemistry can generate similar temperature trends.

**Work:** Compare a metallic conductor, a doped semiconductor and a disordered conducting material using actual state, temperature range, geometry and uncertainty. Test at least two transport models against held-out conditions, and describe a structural or spectroscopic measurement that could distinguish their mechanisms.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
